[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: The video begins with the creator speaking, overlaid with a ComfyUI workflow showing nodes and a generated image of a woman on stairs.
AUDIO: 'What a time to be alive in the open source AI world. Just as we are all catching our breath from the massive Ideogram 4 release this June 2026, another blockbuster open source model has just dropped and everyone is talking about it. Creater 2. It\'s fast, it\'s'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lightweight, and the image quality is outstanding. Today, I\'m giving you the complete guide to mastering this highly flexible, [music] fast generating model inside ComfyUI. I\'ll teach you how to set up an advanced'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'local uncensored LLM prompting system for Creater 2. I\'ll also show you step-by-step how to train your very own consistent character LoRA using AI toolkit so you can put yourself or any'

[00:40] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI interface is shown, displaying "Model Links" and "Model Storage Location" sections, detailing where to place model files.
AUDIO: 'character directly into the scene. If you\'re excited to start, go ahead and drop a like on this video, subscribe for more advanced ComfyUI guides, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and ring that bell to get notified the second my next tutorial drops. We\'ll'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start with a completely blank canvas. To make things easy, I\'m first adding a simple note node here. This node contains the direct links to download every single Creater 2 model you need. Just right-click, save each model into'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'its correct folder within your main ComfyUI directory, and you\'re good to go. Now, let\'s build the foundation. We need three basic loader nodes. First, a load diffusion model node,'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then a load clip node, and finally, a VAE loader node. If you\'ve just downloaded the models and they aren\'t showing up in the drop-down menus, don\'t worry. Just tap the R key'

[01:32] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI interface shows "Load Diffusion Model," "Load CLIP," and "Load VAE" nodes, with dropdowns for selecting models.
AUDIO: 'on your keyboard to refresh ComfyUI and it will instantly detect the new files. For our diffusion model, we are choosing the Creater 2 FP8 Turbo version, not the raw one. In our clip loader, we\'ll load'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Kwen 3 VL 4 billion FP8 scaled version. And for the VAE loader, we\'re starting with the Kwen image VAE. Though keep in mind, we\'re going to swap this out later for an alternative VAE that I'

[01:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'found works much better for this specific model. Next, we need to set up our prompts. Let\'s load two clip text encoder nodes, one for our positive prompt, and one for our negative prompt. Connect both of these to our clip model.'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In the negative prompt, I\'ll add a simple term like watermark to keep our outputs clean. With our models and text encoders ready, we need a K sampler to process all this data. Connect the model input, the positive'

[02:26] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI workflow is expanded, showing connections between "Load Diffusion Model," "CLIP Text Encode (Prompt)," "KSampler," and "VAE Decode" nodes.
AUDIO: 'conditioning, and the negative conditioning to the sampler. We also need to define our canvas size, so let\'s load an empty latent image node and connect it to the latent input of the K sampler. This lets us specify the exact'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'width and height of our generation. Inside the K sampler, let\'s set the seed to fixed. This gives us much better control. If we want to tweak the prompt slightly, but keep the same composition,'

[02:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we can do that easily. Because this is a turbo model, we only need eight steps to get a highly detailed image. We\'ll set the CFG scale to one. This model pairs incredibly well with the Euler simple'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sampler, though you can definitely experiment with other samplers like Euler SDE later on. To turn that raw latent data into a viewable image, we\'ll add a VAE decode node and connect it to our VAE loader.'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Finally, let\'s attach an image preview node so we can see what we generate. That is the entire basic pipeline for Kraevel 2 Turbo. Now, we just need a positive prompt. I\'ve prepared a'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detailed one beforehand. Kraevel 2 is very flexible and works with short, medium, or long prompts. Later in the video, I\'ll show you how to automate this prompting process using a local uncensored LLM. Let\'s set our resolution'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the standard 1024 by 1024 and hit Q prompt. Look at that. We ran into an error. This is a common issue related to the conditioning and the Qwen 3 VL model. To'

[03:57] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI workflow is shown, with the "KSampler" node highlighted, indicating the generation process is active.
AUDIO: 'fix it, we need to set the correct type on our clip loader. Change the type drop down to Craya 2. If you don\'t see that option or you still get an error, it simply means your ComfyUI setup is a bit'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outdated. You can easily fix this by updating via the ComfyUI manager or running a git pull in your terminal. Let\'s run it again. There we go. It\'s generating.'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The turbo model is incredibly fast. In terms of speed and VRAM usage, it feels very similar to Z image turbo or similar fast models. And here is our first generated image. For a first try, Craya'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2 handled this prompt beautifully. The composition is spot on. An eye-level medium shot of a redhead woman sitting on rough concrete stairs with a concrete wall on the right and some greenery in'

[04:48] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI interface displays a generated image of a woman on stairs, with the prompt text visible below it.
AUDIO: 'the upper left. It has a great relaxed lifestyle photo vibe. Fortunately, our model handled the lighting beautifully. It\'s soft daylight coming from the front and upper left'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'casting very gentle shadows. The face looks natural without that artificial plastic sheen. The smile is genuinely believable and the hair has realistic variation. The outfit also matches our'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'description, a white cropped tank top, black wide-leg trousers and tan strapped sandals all with natural-looking fabric folds. What really impressed me is how well it handled the hands and feet.'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Anatomy is usually a weak spot for these models. But her hand resting on her cheek looks natural. The toes and sandals are clean and her body feels realistically weighted on the stairs'

[05:38] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI workflow is shown, with the "Empty Latent Image" node's width and height parameters being adjusted.
AUDIO: 'rather than looking like she\'s floating. Here is my first big tip for working with Craya 2. Change the VAE from the Kwen image to the 1 2.1. It is fully supported and does a much cleaner job'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with overall image coherence. You might not notice a massive shift immediately, but over multiple generations, the difference becomes very clear. Now, let\'s push the resolution. This'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model is capable of generating high-resolution images directly without needing external upscaling workflows. Let\'s set it to 2048 by 2048'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and run it again. What stands out here is how well the composition holds together at higher resolutions. Many models start breaking down when pushed this far, introducing waxy skin, distorted hands, or double'

[06:28] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI workflow shows the "Empty Latent Image" node with adjusted dimensions, and the generated image on the right.
AUDIO: 'heads. Here, the image actually becomes cleaner and more defined. The face is sharper, the individual strands of hair are clearer, and the skin texture looks incredibly natural.'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can also see a massive improvement in the textures of the environment. The concrete stairs and wall have much richer detail, the folds in the dark trousers are more defined, and the hands and feet still look structurally sound.'

[06:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The model uses those extra pixels to add real depth, clarity, and realism without making it look overprocessed. You can easily switch this to a vertical aspect'

[07:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ratio for mobile-friendly shots, or use a wide landscape ratio. The model is highly flexible with its'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dimensions. Now, let\'s take things a step further and add a custom Laura trained on my own photos to generate consistent images of myself. I\'ll add a load Laura node and and my custom character Laura file.'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Don\'t worry, I\'m going to show you exactly how to train your own high-quality Krea 2 character Laura in just a moment. So, keep watching. We\'ll connect the model output from our Laura'

[07:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'node to the K sampler and pass the clip output through to our clip text encoders. Let\'s run the exact same prompt and see how it looks. The Laura did a fantastic job of'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capturing my likeness. The facial structure, skin tone, and overall appearance feel distinct and recognizable rather than a generic AI face. Even better, it doesn\'t overpower the rest of the prompt. We still have'

[08:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the exact same pose, the concrete stairs, the white top, the black trousers, and the soft lighting. A good character Laura should inject identity without ruining the scene layout, and that\'s exactly what\'s happening here.'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, for the advanced setup, I\'m going to show you how to connect an Ollama node to load a local Gemma 4 LLM. This will process a reference image using a custom system prompt I created, automatically generating a highly'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detailed text prompt tailored specifically for Krea 2 Turbo. I won\'t go into detail on how to install Ollama or Gemma 4 in ComfyUI today, but you can check out my dedicated video on that'

[08:54] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI interface shows an "Ollama Connectivity" node and "Ollama Chat" node, with a reference image loaded below. A "Visit My Ko-fi Shop" button is visible.
AUDIO: 'right after this one. To set this up, let\'s load a load image node to bring in a reference photo. This won\'t copy the image exactly like a ControlNet would, but it will analyze the composition and style to give us a highly similar'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'result. Next, we\'ll load an Ollama chat node and an Ollama connectivity node. I\'ll paste my custom system prompt into the top text box, and we\'ll leave the'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lower user prompt box empty for a moment. I\'ll explain how we can use that in a bit. By the way, if you want to skip the manual setup, you can download this entire workflow from my Kofi page for free, or you can choose to support'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the channel there. The link is right down in the description. Let\'s connect our reference image to the Olama chat node, and then route the output text directly into our positive clip text encoder. This means the prompt generated'

[09:48] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI workflow is shown with the Ollama nodes integrated, and a generated image on the right.
AUDIO: 'by Gemma 4 will feed directly into our K sampler automatically. Make sure you have Olama running in the background of your machine, so it can load the Gemma 4 model. Let\'s run the workflow. This will'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take just a moment longer, since the LLM has to analyze the image and write the prompt first. If you don\'t want to run a local LLM, you can copy my system prompt and paste it into Grok, then copy the'

[10:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resulting text back into your positive prompt box manually. Looking at this test, the workflow did an incredible job preserving the character identity, the outfit style, the pose, the chair, and the overall'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'composition. The only tiny detail it missed was the exact hair color, but that is very easy to resolve. We can use the lower user prompt box in our Olama node to override'

[10:39] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The ComfyUI workflow displays a "Conditioning Creati Rebalance" node connected between the CLIP Text Encoder and KSampler.
AUDIO: 'any details analyzed by the vision model. For example, if I write, "Make the woman with fiery red shoulder-length hair and green eyes," the LLM will prioritize those specific details in the final'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'output, regardless of what\'s in the reference image. Here is a quick tip. This model does have some built-in safety filters that'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'might block certain prompts. You can easily bypass this by using a custom Laura called Conditioning Creati Rebalance. You can grab that from GitHub, and I\'ve also linked it on my Coffee page. Just connect it directly'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between your clip text encoder and the K sampler. I won\'t be running it here for obvious reasons, but if you generate that style of content, this is the exact tool you need.'

